        ["A patient with a transdermal fentanyl patch on her upper back asks to have a moist hot pack over that area. What is the best response?",["Avoid heat over the patch; heat speeds drug absorption","Apply the hot pack, since heat has no effect on patch delivery","Apply heat but have her remove the patch for the session","Use continuous ultrasound over the patch instead of the heat"],"Heat increases local blood flow and skin permeability, which can speed opioid absorption and cause an overdose. Patients should never remove or adjust prescribed patches for PT."],
        ["An older client takes nine prescription drugs, and his falls have increased over the past month. Which explanation is most useful to consider first?",["Polypharmacy and adverse drug effects","Normal aging alone, with no medication link at all","Poor cardiovascular endurance as the only cause","Excessive protein intake from his daily supplements"],"Taking many drugs raises the risk of interactions, sedation, and orthostatic hypotension. A medication review with the prescriber is an important fall-prevention step."],
        ["Which statement correctly distinguishes potency from efficacy?",["Potency is dose needed; efficacy is maximum effect","Potency is maximum effect; efficacy is dose needed","Both describe how long a drug's action lasts","Efficacy describes side effects, potency benefit"],"A more potent drug works at a lower dose, but a less potent drug can still have greater efficacy if its maximum effect is larger."],
        ["A drug with a half-life of 8 hours is stopped. About how long until it is essentially eliminated from the body?",["About 32-40 hours","About 8 hours","About 4 hours","About 7 days"],"A drug is essentially eliminated after about 4-5 half-lives, so 4-5 times 8 hours is roughly 32-40 hours."],
        ["Which route of administration bypasses the first-pass effect?",["Transdermal patch","Oral tablet","Oral liquid","Swallowed capsule"],"Transdermal, sublingual, inhaled, and IV routes enter the systemic circulation without first passing through the liver. All oral routes go through the portal circulation."],
        ["A patient on a long-term opioid needs a higher dose to get the same pain relief he had months ago. Which term BEST describes this?",["Tolerance","Addiction","Bioavailability","First-pass effect"],"Tolerance is a reduced response to the same dose after repeated exposure. Addiction is a behavioral disorder of compulsive use, which is a separate concept."],
        ["An 86-year-old woman feels groggy and unsteady every morning after a bedtime sleeping pill. Which age-related change MOST likely explains this?",["Slower clearance and more body fat prolong the drug's effect","Faster liver metabolism inactivates the drug too quickly","Higher kidney blood flow concentrates the drug in plasma","Increased muscle mass stores the drug and releases it later"],"Older adults often have reduced renal and hepatic clearance and a higher fat-to-water ratio, so fat-soluble sedatives last longer and cause next-day hangover effects and falls."],
        ["Which statement about a drug with a narrow therapeutic window is MOST accurate?",["Small changes in level can cause toxicity or loss of effect","It can be safely doubled whenever symptoms worsen","It has no side effects within its normal dosing range","It never needs blood level or lab monitoring"],"When the effective and toxic levels are close together, minor changes in dose, interactions, or kidney function can push the drug out of range, so drugs like warfarin and digoxin need monitoring."]
