        "<b>NSAIDs</b> (ibuprofen, naproxen, aspirin) inhibit <b>COX enzymes</b>, reducing prostaglandins. Effects are analgesic, anti-inflammatory, and antipyretic.",
        "<b>NSAID adverse effects</b>: <b>GI irritation, ulcers, and bleeding</b> (dark tarry stools and coffee-ground vomit are warning signs), kidney damage, raised blood pressure and fluid retention, and increased bleeding risk, especially combined with anticoagulants. COX-2 selective drugs (celecoxib) cause less GI irritation but carry cardiovascular risk.",
        "<b>Aspirin</b> irreversibly inhibits platelet COX for the life of the platelet, which is why low-dose aspirin is used for heart attack and stroke prevention.",
        "<b>Acetaminophen</b>: analgesic and antipyretic but <b>not anti-inflammatory</b>, and gentle on the stomach. Overdose causes <b>liver toxicity</b>, and the risk rises with alcohol use. Watch for hidden acetaminophen in combination products.",
        "<b>Opioids</b> (morphine, oxycodone, hydrocodone, fentanyl) act on mu receptors. Side effects include <b>sedation, respiratory depression, constipation, nausea, dizziness, and orthostatic hypotension</b>, all raising fall risk. Tolerance and physical dependence develop with long-term use.",
        "<b>Corticosteroids</b> (prednisone, dexamethasone, injected cortisone) are potent anti-inflammatories. Long-term systemic use causes <b>osteoporosis, muscle wasting (proximal myopathy), skin fragility, poor wound healing, hyperglycemia, and immune suppression</b>.",
